BEGIN:VCALENDAR
VERSION:2.0
PRODID:-//WebMS Intra//Test fixture//EN
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:What this proves: a whole day is not always 24 hours, so the length of a
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:whole-day event and the day it ends on must both be worked out by the
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:calendar, never by counting 86,400 seconds per day.
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:THE DATES ARE THE WHOLE POINT. In London the clocks go BACK at 02:00 on
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:Sunday 25 October 2026, which makes that day 25 hours long, and FORWARD at
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:01:00 on Sunday 28 March 2027, which makes that day 23 hours long. Every
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:other whole-day fixture here sits in December, June or mid-October, where
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:no clock change happens and a reader that counted seconds would look
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:perfectly right. Do not move these events to a quieter month: doing so
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:would leave the checks passing and stop them proving anything at all.
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:The two mistakes bite on different nights, which is why both are here.
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:A LENGTH counted as seconds divided by 86,400 is short on the 23-hour
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:night (47 real hours across 27-28 March reads as one day, not two). An
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:END counted as the start plus N times 86,400 seconds lands an hour early
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:on the 25-hour night, so a run STARTING on 25 October loses its last day.
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:The last FOUR events are about the REPEAT ENGINE rather than the length.
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:It walks from one date to the next, and a step of a day taken as 86,400
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:seconds lands an hour short on the 25-hour night. For a whole-day event
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:that puts the step back on the day it started from, so a date is lost.
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:The daily one loses 27 October. The Saturday one uses WKST=SU (the week
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:starts on Sunday), which American calendars are believed to do commonly
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:(not measured here - no real export exists in this repository), because that is
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:what puts the clock change INSIDE the week rather than at its far end - it
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:loses Saturday 31 October and offers 8 November in its place.
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:The FINAL PAIR is about the other end of that same engine, and about the
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:MARCH night rather than the October one. Before a weekly rule looks for its
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:days it steps back to the first day of the week the event starts in. From
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:Saturday 3 April 2027 with WKST=SU that is six days back, onto Sunday 28
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:March - the 23-hour day. Taken as six blocks of 86,400 seconds the step
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:lands an hour EARLY, at eleven at night on Saturday the 27th, so the
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:seven-day week the rule then searches begins a day early and, just as
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:important, ENDS a day early - pushing that week's Saturday out of it.
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:INTERVAL=2 is what makes that visible. The pushed-out Saturday is not
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:picked up by the next week searched, because at INTERVAL=2 the next week
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:searched starts a FORTNIGHT later - so every date after the first comes
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:back a week early, on the off weeks. At INTERVAL=1 the pushed-out Saturday
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:turns up in the very next week and the dates look right, which is why the
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:weekly twin is here as well and is COUNTED - the start date is then offered
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:twice, counts twice against COUNT, and the duplicate is quietly dropped, so
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:a three-date series comes back with two. Do not change INTERVAL, WKST or
X-WEBMS-FIXTURE-NOTE:the month of this pair, and do not merge the two into one event.
